174	    /**
175	     * Normalize a telephone to its canonical E.164 stored form. This is the single source of
176	     * truth for how a telephone is compared and persisted, so inputs that differ only in
177	     * formatting (spaces, dashes, brackets) are treated as the same number.
178	     *
179	     * <p>The rules are:
180	     * <ul>
181	     *   <li>spaces, dashes and brackets are stripped;</li>
182	     *   <li>a leading {@code '+'} and its country code are kept as given;</li>
183	     *   <li>otherwise country code {@code '+61'} is assumed and a single leading {@code '0'}
184	     *       is dropped from the national digits;</li>
185	     *   <li>the result must have 8 to 15 digits after the {@code '+'};</li>
186	     *   <li>for a recognized country code the national number (the digits after the country
187	     *       code) must have the length that code requires: {@code '+61'} requires 9 national
188	     *       digits and {@code '+1'} requires 10.</li>
189	     * </ul>
190	     * For example {@code "0412 345 678"} becomes {@code "+61412345678"}. Already-normalized
191	     * values are returned unchanged.
192	     *
193	     * @param telephone the raw telephone, possibly {@code null}
194	     * @return the E.164 form, or {@code null} if the input is {@code null} or cannot form a
195	     *     valid E.164 number
